{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets every one of these acceptance lines:\n1. index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\n2. padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\n3. README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\n4. npm test passes\n\nSteps:\n1. Read index.js, README.md, package.json and test/strutil.test.js. index.js uses ES module syntax (`export function`), and `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n2. In index.js, add and export `padLeft(s, width, fill)`. Put a short doc comment above it, the same way the other functions have one. It must pad `s` on the left with `fill` until the result is exactly `width` characters long. When `fill` is not given (undefined), use a single space. When `s.length >= width`, return `s` unchanged. Example: padLeft('42', 5, '0') === '00042', padLeft('42', 5) === '   42', padLeft('12345', 3, '0') === '12345'. The simplest way is `String.prototype.padStart`. Leave the existing exports alone.\n3. In README.md, make sure there is a `## name(args)` section for EVERY function index.js exports, not only the new one. Note: README.md currently has sections for capitalize and truncate but NOT for slugify, even though index.js exports slugify. Add a `## slugify(s)` section that describes what it does (lower case, runs of non-alphanumeric characters become single dashes, leading and trailing dashes removed). Add a `## padLeft(s, width, fill)` section that describes the padding, the default space fill and the unchanged result when s is already long enough, and gives the invoice example 42 -> 00042. Match the style of the sections already there. Keep them above the `## Tests` section.\n4. In test/strutil.test.js, import padLeft and add tests for: zero fill ('42', 5, '0' -> '00042'), default space fill ('42', 5 -> '   42'), s equal to width ('abc', 3, '0' -> 'abc') and s longer than width ('abcdef', 3, '0' -> 'abcdef').\n5. Run `npm test` and confirm every test passes. Then go back through every `export` in index.js and confirm each exported function has a matching README.md section.\n6. Open the pull request, linking https://github.com/o/strutil/issues/7.","verifier":"Check the pull request's head checkout. Run every command from the repository root. Run each check below in order and give each one PASS or FAIL with the evidence (the command and its output).\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"index.js exports padLeft(s, width, fill), which pads s on the left with fill (a space when fill is not given) to width characters\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; const p=m.padLeft; console.log(typeof p, JSON.stringify([p('42',5,'0'), p('42',5), p('',3,'x'), p('7',4,'*')]))\"\nPASS: the output is `function [\"00042\",\"   42\",\"xxx\",\"***7\"]`. That means padLeft is exported, pads on the left with fill up to width characters, and uses a space when fill is not given. Also read index.js at the head and confirm padLeft is declared with the parameters (s, width, fill).\nFAIL: padLeft is not exported (typeof is not `function`, or the import fails), any output value is different, or the declaration does not take s, width, fill.\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"padLeft returns s unchanged when s is already width characters or longer\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import {padLeft as p} from './index.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify([p('abc',3,'0'), p('abcdef',3,'0'), p('abc',3), p('abcdef',2)]))\"\nPASS: the output is `[\"abc\",\"abcdef\",\"abc\",\"abcdef\"]`.\nFAIL: any value is different (for example, truncated or padded), or the command throws.\n\nCheck 3 — expect line: \"README.md has a section for every function index.js exports\"\nRun:\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import * as m from './index.js'; console.log(Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function').join(' '))\"\nto list the exported functions. Then run `grep -n '^#' README.md` to list the README headings. Read README.md at the head.\nPASS: every exported function name listed (at least capitalize, slugify, truncate, padLeft) has its own README.md section, meaning a heading naming that function with text under it that describes it.\nFAIL: any exported function has no README.md section. Note that the base README lacked a slugify section, so check slugify in particular.\n\nCheck 4 — expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: `npm test`\nPASS: it exits with code 0 and reports no failing tests.\nFAIL: it exits non-zero or reports any failing test.\n\nAdvisory (not part of any PASS/FAIL):\n- See whether test/strutil.test.js has padLeft tests for the default fill and the already-long case.\n- See whether the padLeft README section mentions the invoice example (42 -> 00042)."}